---
// The first steps, in English. Each command was run as written, in a PTY, on the date the
// status page gives: Notes starts, with and without the simulated latency; an edit of
// NoteList.tsx shows on the screen after the dev server's rebuild; `init` makes an app
// that installs, type-checks and starts.
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import CopyCommand from "../../components/CopyCommand.astro";
import { doc, repo, source } from "../../lib/links";

const EDIT = "examples/notes/components/NoteList.tsx";
---

<Page
  title="Getting started"
  description="Run the Notes example of airtty, make a first visible change, slow the network down and create your own terminal app with React Server Components."
>
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p>
    You will run Notes, the example app of the home page, change what it draws, and create an app
    of your own. Both sides, client and server, run on your machine.
  </p>

  <h2>What you need</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>macOS or Linux, and git.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bun 1.4.2</strong>, the version the repository pins:
    </li>
  </ul>
  <CopyCommand lines={["curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash -s bun-v1.4.2"]} />
  <p>
    Nothing is published on a registry yet: you work from a clone of the repository.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Run Notes</h2>
  <CopyCommand lines={[`git clone ${repo} && cd airtty`, "bun install --frozen-lockfile", "bun run dev"]} />
  <p>
    One command builds both programs and starts them: the server, with a SQLite database created as
    <code>notes.sqlite</code> in the current directory, and the terminal client. In the list, move
    with the arrows and open a note with <kbd>Enter</kbd>. In a note, <kbd>Enter</kbd> or
    <kbd>Ctrl+S</kbd> saves, <kbd>Esc</kbd> goes back to the list. <kbd>Ctrl+C</kbd> quits and
    restores the terminal.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Make a first change</h2>
  <p>
    Leave it running and open <a href={source(EDIT)}><code>{EDIT}</code></a>, a client component.
    Change the heading:
  </p>
  <pre><code><span class="comment">-</span>      &lt;text&gt;YOUR NOTES&lt;/text&gt;
<span class="comment">+</span>      &lt;text&gt;MY NOTES&lt;/text&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Save the file. The dev server rebuilds and restarts both processes, and the client reopens the
    same screen, with the new heading, in a couple of seconds. The route, the history and the text
    of named fields come back; state held only in memory does not: there is no Fast Refresh.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Slow the network down</h2>
  <p>Quit with <kbd>Ctrl+C</kbd>, then start again with half a second added to every request:</p>
  <CopyCommand lines={["AIRTTY_LATENCY_MS=500 bun run dev"]} />
  <p>
    Open a note: a loading screen shows at once, drawn by the client, while the page travels. Type,
    save with <kbd>Enter</kbd>, and keep typing: the keys are handled in the terminal while the save
    waits for the server. The delay is simulated per request, not a network emulation.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Create your own app</h2>
  <p>From the root of the clone:</p>
  <CopyCommand
    lines={["bun packages/airtty/src/cli.ts init ../my-app", "cd ../my-app", "bun install", "bun run dev"]}
  />
  <p>
    The starter is a small notes app to reshape, in one codebase. Its <code>airtty</code> dependency
    points at your clone, which has to stay where it is.
  </p>
  <dl>
    <dt>app/</dt>
    <dd>
      Routes as files: <code>page.tsx</code> pages render on the server, <code>layout.tsx</code> and
      <code>loading.tsx</code> are client components.
    </dd>
    <dt>components/</dt>
    <dd>Client components, marked <code>"use client"</code>: they handle the keyboard.</dd>
    <dt>actions/</dt>
    <dd>
      Server Functions, marked <code>"use server"</code>: the client calls them like functions.
    </dd>
    <dt>server/</dt>
    <dd>Code that only the server runs: the database, queries, checks.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>
    <code>bun run check</code> type-checks it; <code>bun run build</code> builds both programs for
    production.
  </p>

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="/guide/">The guided tour</a>, in French: what happens between a key and the database.
    </li>
    <li>
      The reference, in French, in the repository: <a href={doc("API.md")}>API</a>,
      <a href={doc("ROUTER.md")}>routing</a>, <a href={doc("CACHE.md")}>cache</a>,
      <a href={doc("DISTRIBUTION.md")}>distribution</a>.
    </li>
    <li><a href="/examples/">The examples</a>, each one command away.</li>
  </ul>
</Page>
